@inject DeskToasts Toasts

@* ==========================================================================
   Notices
   ========================================================================== *@

<PanelSection Title="Notices">
<p class="empty">
    <span class="mono">&lt;Note&gt;</span> is the ambient tier: it sits beside the thing it
    describes and stays as long as the thing is true. No dismiss control, no timer — if it
    needs acknowledging it is an alert, and if it should vanish it is a toast.
</p>

<DeskStack Gap="DeskSpace.Space2">
    <Note>
        A note carries context the operator needs but did not ask for.
    </Note>
    <Note State="DeskState.Warning">
        A warning carries a cost. State what it is, and what to do instead.
    </Note>
</DeskStack>

<p class="empty">
    Only <span class="mono">DeskState.Warning</span> changes the skin, because a cost is the
    one thing an operator has to act on. ADR 0016's rule holds inside the box too: the hue
    never carries the message on its own — the sentence says what the cost is, and the wash
    only makes it findable.
</p>

<DeskRow Gap="DeskSpace.Space2" Align="DeskAlign.Center">
    <span class="meta">At word scale, inside a row or cell:</span>
    <span class="mono">
        <Note Inline State="DeskState.Warning">62%</Note>
    </span>
</DeskRow>

</PanelSection>

<PanelSection Title="Tooltip">
<p class="empty">
    A sentence of explanation beside the thing it explains, raised by hover and by keyboard focus
    alike after a short pause — for what a <code>title</code> cannot carry, such as two lines.
    Where one short phrase will do, <code>title</code> stays. It describes its trigger and never
    renames it: the link below is still announced by its own name.
</p>

<DeskRow Gap="DeskSpace.Space6" Align="DeskAlign.Center">
    <DeskTooltip Text="@_linkTip">
        <DeskLink Destination="details" OnClick="@(() => { })">North region</DeskLink>
    </DeskTooltip>
    <DeskTooltip Text="@_textTip" Placement="DeskTooltipPlacement.Bottom">
        <span class="meta">14 of 21 reviewed</span>
    </DeskTooltip>
</DeskRow>

<p class="empty">
    On plain text it is the pointer's alone — text takes no focus — so it may add to what the
    text says, never be the only place a thing is said.
</p>

<p class="empty">
    When the explanation is a small table rather than a sentence, it takes the structured shape:
    a semibold <code>Title</code>, a <code>Caption</code> held to a measure, <code>Rows</code> of
    label and value in tabular numerals with a quiet qualifier, the row it is about by weight, and
    a <code>Footnote</code> saying where the rest are — it cannot be clicked into. A screen reader
    hears <code>Text</code>, or the parts as plain lines.
</p>

<DeskRow Gap="DeskSpace.Space6" Align="DeskAlign.Center">
    <DeskTooltip Title="Highest reading" Caption="@_tableTipCaption" Rows="@_tableTipRows"
                 Footnote="+4 more in Regions">
        <span class="meta" tabindex="0">North 412</span>
    </DeskTooltip>
</DeskRow>

</PanelSection>

<PanelSection Title="Checks">
<p class="empty">
    Advisory facts before a step is taken, as a grouped section: a mark, one sentence, and the
    remedy key on the row where there is one. The mark's hue is the second channel; the
    sentence carries the meaning, so the list still reads with the colour taken away.
</p>

<CheckList Items="_checks" Title="Before you send" />

</PanelSection>

<PanelSection Title="Toasts">
<p class="empty">
    The transient tier. A toast reports that something finished while you were looking
    somewhere else. A success, an info or a neutral notice goes after five seconds, a warning
    after eight; a failure, and anything with a key, stays until it is pressed or dismissed. The
    header's <span class="mono">DeskToastStack</span> draws them in the bar's free stretch, as
    one capsule: the count's key, the newest in front cut to one line, the rest as edges stepped
    out to its right; pointing, focusing or pressing the count drops the whole list beneath it.
    Where the stretch is narrow it is the key alone. The operator reads the stack as Activities —
    press a key below and watch the header. Panels take <span class="mono">DeskToasts</span>, never
    <span class="mono">ISnackbar</span>.
</p>

<DeskRow Gap="DeskSpace.Space2">
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Success("Evaluated — every source inside its SLO."))">
        Success
    </DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Info("Layout saved to this browser."))">
        Info
    </DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Warn("Pull finished — 12 pulls left in today's allowance."))">
        Warn
    </DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Fail("Scheduled pull failed — the provider timed out."))">
        Fail
    </DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Show("Nothing to report."))">
        Neutral
    </DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small"
                OnClick="@(() => Toasts.Added("Added a row to the list.", "Show", () => { }))">
        With a key
    </DeskButton>
    <DeskButton Size="DeskKeySize.Small"
                OnClick="RaiseSeveral">
        Several at once
    </DeskButton>
</DeskRow>
</PanelSection>

@code {
    /// <summary>Four notices in a burst, so the stack's layering and its count can be seen.</summary>
    private void RaiseSeveral()
    {
        Toasts.Info("Layout saved to this browser.");
        Toasts.Success("Evaluated — every source inside its SLO.");
        Toasts.Warn("Finished, with two sources slow.");
        Toasts.Fail("A scheduled run failed — the provider timed out.");
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- tooltip ---

    private const string _linkTip = "Opens the region's details.\nIts history is one tab over.";
    private const string _textTip = "Reviewed means someone other than the owner has read it.";
    private const string _tableTipCaption = "The latest count, taken 40 minutes ago.\nTarget 380 · set for the quarter";

    private static readonly DeskTooltipRow[] _tableTipRows =
    [
        new("North", "412", "+8.4%", Emphasis: true),
        new("East", "398", "+4.7%"),
        new("West", "371", "−2.4%"),
        new("South", "366", "−3.7%"),
    ];

    // ----------------------------------------------------------------- checks ---

    private IReadOnlyList<Check> _checks =>
    [
        new(DeskState.Positive, "Every item has an owner."),
        new(DeskState.Warning, "Two items have no due date.", "Set dates", () =>
        {
            Toasts.Info("A remedy opens where the fix is made.");
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }),
        new(DeskState.Negative, "One item is over its limit, so it cannot be sent as it is.")
    ];
}
